1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,552
Reactive programming introduced
publishers.

2
00:00:03,672 --> 00:00:15,264
Most harbor lookups are not streams of
thousands of rows — they are “give me this
vessel by MMSI,” “load this berth
assignment,” “call this registry once.”
Reactor’s type for that is Mono<T>:

3
00:00:15,384 --> 00:00:20,064
a publisher that completes with zero or
one item, or with an error.

4
00:00:20,184 --> 00:00:34,439
Think of Mono as a lazy asynchronous
Optional with operators — but do not
implement it by wrapping blocking calls in
Mono.just.
Mono.just(repo.findByMmsi(mmsi)) still
blocks the caller thread before the Mono
even exists.

5
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:48,672
Prefer sources that are non-blocking, or
defer blocking work explicitly. Look at
this java for a moment. Operator
vocabulary you should say out loud. map
transforms the item synchronously.

6
00:00:48,792 --> 00:01:03,359
flatMap chains another Mono or Flux when
each item needs an async call — the
reactive replacement for nested callbacks.
switchIfEmpty handles the zero-item case.
timeout, retry, onErrorResume shape time
and failure.

7
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:19,200
zipWhen / zipWith combine parallel
lookups. then ignores the payload and
waits for completion — useful for deletes.
Cardinality is the contract. If you
accidentally emit two items into a Mono,
Reactor signals an error.

8
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:32,760
If your repository can return many
positions, you wanted Flux. Choosing Mono
documents intent: WebFlux treats Mono as a
single JSON object body, not an array.
Empty is not null.

9
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:47,400
A completed empty Mono means “no value.”
Mapping without guarding empty keeps
emptiness. Turning empty into an error
with switchIfEmpty(Mono.error(...)) is how
you express 404-style vessel misses at the
reactive layer.

10
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:57,000
Returning Mono.justOrEmpty(optional)
bridges imperative code carefully;
returning null from a reactive adapter is
not the same and usually breaks callers.

11
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:07,103
A misconception is calling .block() on a
Mono inside WebFlux request handling to
“make it simple.” That reintroduces thread
blocking on the event loop.

12
00:02:07,224 --> 00:02:13,319
Blocking belongs at edges you control —
tests sometimes, or a narrow adapter — not
in the hot path.

13
00:02:13,439 --> 00:02:20,544
Another misconception is nesting subscribe
inside map instead of flatMap — that
breaks backpressure and error propagation.

14
00:02:20,663 --> 00:02:27,287
A third is treating Mono as a servlet-
style injected dependency rather than a
publisher you compose and return.

15
00:02:27,407 --> 00:02:42,048
Today we treated Mono as the 0..1 reactive
publisher: compose with flatMap, handle
empty, signal errors, return it to the web
layer instead of blocking. Live AIS maps
need 0..n. That publisher is Flux.
